   "API9 (Improper Inventory Management) gets its own lesson because it is the precondition of every other control in this level: you cannot put a per-object check, a rate budget, or a gateway rule on an API you do not know exists. The inventory discipline has four parts — DISCOVER (gateway logs, DNS, code repos, cloud accounts: four independent nets, because each misses some), CLASSIFY (owner, data class, auth state, exposure), CONTROL (bring the found API under the level's standard or retire it), and PREVENT (a paved path: new APIs are born registered, through the gateway, or they do not ship). The builder below assembles a simulated inventory from three discovery sources and flags what no source's owner claimed.",
   "Work the example the professional way: read it completely before running anything, write down the output you predict, run it exactly as written, and reconcile any difference before you change a single character. The why-panel under the code then answers, in order: why this code exists, what each part does, why the output came out as it did, what it means for real work, and what a wrong run looks like.",
